on lock is explicit and OECD's own aggregate matches it): A = 0.75(0.80) + 0.25(0.35) = 0.69.\nCONJUNCT B (China headline CPI < 1.50%/yr, 2027-36). Here the red-team landed a genuine hit on the Mechanism panelist's 0.78, which rested on an N=1 Japan reference class treated as near-automatic. Real content in the attack: China's own 2000-2019 average was ~2.6%; food is ~18-20% of the NBS basket and supplies nearly all the variance, so one +5% headline food year adds ~+0.5pp to a decade average and two pork-cycle peaks are the expectation, not the tail; the 2A fiscal block (2.9T RMB of pension/social-security subsidy, worker/retiree 4.4 -> 2.8) is a transfer-financed consumption floor under a service-heavy retiree basket, which is a services-price support and not only a drag; the 2026 and 2031 basket rebases bias prints up. Against him: the locked 2A contraction (-60M over 2026-35) is given fact and is the configuration under which 3 of 3 Japanese decade windows cleared, and his 0.50 discards it. I take the Fermi panelist's path arithmetic (0.3% in 2027-29, 1.2% in 2030-32, 1.8% in 2033-36 -> 1.17% decade average, sd 0.60 -> 0.71) and shade it down for the one-sided food-cycle asymmetry and policy reflation intent: B = 0.64.\nCORRELATION. Mildly negative, as all five panelists agree, but weak: 0.78pp of my conjunct-A central value is the emerging-OECD composition term, which is orthogonal to Chinese goods prices, and ~70-75% of the remainder is services. rho = -0.08 -> P(A|B) = 0.69 x 0.94 = 0.65.\nJOINT = 0.65 x 0.64 = 0.416. ANSWER 42. Note the structure, not the level: I am far above the Reference-Class and Mechanism panelists on the OECD leg (0.69 vs 0.35-0.53) and below the Mechanism and Fermi panelists on the China leg (0.64 vs 0.70-0.78). Anyone reading 42 as agreement with the Auditor's 49 or the red-team's 40 is reading a coincidence of two offsetting disagreements.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04